  * Sanity: the unweighted/discard family is exactly parity-invariant (max |effect| 0.0000 over 3696 cells), confirming the audit instrument itself is not manufacturing an effect.
  * AIP-gated away from the tie (f != 0.5): 4 of 462 cells clear the floor (0.9%), largest |effect| 0.135. Self-vote share is not detectable as a general property away from the tie, but it is not exactly zero either.
  * AIP-gated AT f = 0.5: 33 of 66 cells clear the floor, mean signed effect +0.423, max 0.840. 'AIP does not exploit self-vote share' is REFUTED at the exact tie: with the swarm split 50/50 the normalisation of the adversary's own vote decides the outcome.
  * Scope: the audit contrasts matched@0.1 against unmatched under the always_wrong adversary, the least coherent one in the sweep, so the tie regime is where the adversary's own vote carries the whole decision.
  * Consequence for the f = 0.5 anchor: any result stated at the tie must name its parity condition, because the two differ by up to 0.840 on this method. The gate-aware sweep (scripts/phase_gate_aware.py) constructs its aggregators with ParityConfig(0.1), i.e. the matched -- confound-controlled -- condition, so the evasion anchor is not contaminated by this effect.

